{"nodes": [{"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_document", "label": "The Syntax of Early English, Chapters 7-9 (infinitival constructions, easy-to-please, grammaticalization)", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": "Olga Fischer, Ans van Kemenade, Willem Koopman, Wim van der Wurff", "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_for_np_to_v_construction", "label": "Rise of the (for) NP to V construction via VO-order fixing", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Benefactive dative/for-phrase + separate purpose-infinitive reanalyzed into complementizer for + infinitive's own subject once Middle English fixed SVO/adjacent surface order; OE's freer word order (esp. V2 placing the matrix predicate between phrases) blocked the reanalysis."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_aci_ecm_distinction", "label": "AcI-proper vs ECM (believe-type) infinitival constructions in Old English", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "AcI-proper (perception/causative verbs) is native OE; ECM (believe-type) is not attested in native OE prose, only in Latin-calqued glosses -- the native strategy remained a finite \u00fe\u00e6t-clause."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_ecm_rise_middle_english", "label": "Rise of ECM (believe-type) construction in Middle English", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "VO-order fixing supplied the template; Latin borrowing (verba declarandi et cogitandi) supplied the lexical class and to-be copular pattern; Warner's least-saliency principle (verified via the two Wycliffite Bible translations) predicts where the borrowed construction first took root."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_dutch_comparative_evidence", "label": "Comparative evidence from the history of Dutch (7.3)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Dutch had comparable Latin exposure and did develop Latinate AcI, but never fixed VO order and the construction never became a permanent native fixture -- the book's strongest evidence that borrowing/analogy alone are insufficient causes of syntactic change; the independent word-order fixing must 'prepare the bed'."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_easy_to_please_tough_movement", "label": "Modern English easy-to-please / tough-movement as A-bar (wh-)movement", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Four empirical arguments (uniform gap types, long movement, parasitic gaps, expletive it subject) favor wh-movement over an NP-movement analysis."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_oe_easy_to_please_restricted_pattern", "label": "Old English easy-to-please: a single, narrowly restricted pattern (8.3)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "46 of 286 OE adjective+infinitive tokens are easy-to-please; every single gap is a direct-object accusative dependency with zero attested preposition-stranding, against a 4-6-example statistical expectation -- treated as positive evidence the option was ungrammatical, not textual accident."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_oe_easy_to_please_passive_infinitive", "label": "OE easy-to-please as underlyingly a covert passive infinitive (8.4)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Van der Wurff's analysis: no syntactic gap/movement chain in OE -- the matrix subject is base-generated as logical object of a passive infinitive, correctly predicting the total absence of P-stranding since passivizing a preposition's object was not yet a general OE option."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_me_shift_wh_movement", "label": "Middle English shift to a wh-movement analysis of easy-to-please (8.5)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Genuine preposition-stranding first attested c.1400, the same watershed date as OV narrowing (Ch.5) and infinitival changes (Ch.7) -- once the passive-infinitive analysis was no longer learnable from the input, learners reanalyzed via the independently available wh-/operator-movement mechanism."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_grammaticalization_theory_critique", "label": "Authors' critique of grammaticalization as an explanatory mechanism (9.1-9.2)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Grammaticalization (bleaching, gradualness, unidirectionality) is not itself a causal/diachronic force but a descriptive label for a recurring pattern; the two case studies are offered to show that a discrete syntactic change (VO/SVO fixing) triggers each episode, with bleaching accompanying rather than driving the reanalysis."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_have_to_grammaticalization", "label": "Case study: grammaticalization of have to (9.3.1)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Four-stage path (OE free lexical-possession + adjunct infinitive -> OE predicative/factive reading -> ME fixed have+to-infinitive+NP in ambiguous A3 contexts -> eModE full modal-auxiliary operator with hafta contraction). Key correction: obligation-sense frequency stays roughly flat across stages (OE 4/9, ME 10/33, eModE 25/80) -- no monotonic bleaching trend, so the trigger is argued to be structural (VO-order fixing forcing reanalysis of the ambiguous A3 object), not gradual semantic drift."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_jespersens_cycle_negation", "label": "Case study: Jespersen's Cycle in the history of English negation (9.3.2)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Four-stage negative cycle traced via the layered CP>FP>NegP>TP structure: pre-Classical-OE unreduced no/n\u00e6fre in Spec,CP with no V-to-C movement -> Classical OE procliticized Neg0 ne with V-to-C -> reinforcing na in Spec,NegP (multiple sentential negation) -> ME nawiht/nowiht weakens to not, inheriting Spec,NegP as ne erodes -> ME/eModE not reanalyzed as a negative head (did not), completing the cycle. Argued to be driven by the single structural NegP format, not independent semantic bleaching."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_concluding_synthesis", "label": "Concluding synthesis: syntactic and semantic change as partially independent (9.3.3)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Both case studies show syntax and semantics as partially independent processes; authors float but don't endorse Postma's displacement-drives-bleaching idea and the observation that grammaticalization endpoints are disproportionately functional-category heads (T, M, D)."}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_easy_class_adjectives", "label": "OE easy-class adjectives (earfo\u00f0, ea\u00f0e, hefig, leoht, etc.)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_eager_class_adjectives", "label": "OE eager-class adjectives (fus, gearo, geornful)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_habban_have_verb", "label": "OE habban 'to have', source of have to", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_hit_expletive_subject", "label": "OE expletive/dummy subject hit ('it')", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_no_naefre_negators", "label": "Pre-Classical-OE unreduced negators no / n\u00e6fre", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}], "edges": [{"source": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_for_np_to_v_construction", "target": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_document", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_aci_ecm_distinction", "target": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_document", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_ecm_rise_middle_english", "target": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_document", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_dutch_comparative_evidence", "target": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_document", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_easy_to_please_tough_movement", "target": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_document", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "established/075_syntax_early_english_pt03.md", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_oe_easy_to_please_restricted_pattern", "target": "established_075_syntax_early_english_pt03_document", "relation": "references", "confidence": 